on a list of historically bad ideas  japan's decision to bomb po harbour  always gets an honorable mention given  that for japan the outcome was not  exactly a positive one  it's natural to ask what its leaders  hope to achieve so why  why did japan bomb pearl harbor so  before talking about the attack itself  it's important to look at what the  japanese government's long-term plans  were for the country  japan is not a resource-rich country and  so in order to secure long-term security  in terms of things like oil and food  japan sought to expand its borders and  create what was known as the greater  east asia co-prosperity sphere  an empire which would see japan as the  hegemon of asia in the pacific  by 1939 japan had already achieved some  of its expansion and was in control of  manchuria as well as waging war against  china for more territory with respect to  further expansion the japanese  government had two  options the first was the northern plan  favored by the japanese army  this argued for the invasion of the ussr  via manchuria in order to secure the  land and resources of siberia whilst  also grabbing mongolia  and much of china the idea was that  communism was the biggest threat to  japan and that the western powers would  do little to nothing in the defense of  the ussr  plan number two was the southern plan  favored by the navy  this would see japan strike southwards  and take the american and european  colonies in southeast asia notably  indonesia to secure their abundant  resources  this would inevitably lead to  confrontation with the world's imperial  powers and as such was seen as the much  riskier option but to the navy the us  was japan's biggest threat  so when border skirmishes broke out with  the ussr the japanese army didn't  perform all too well and as a result the  japanese government saw the army as  incapable of fulfilling the northern  plan  whereas the navy was still seen as  incredibly prestigious and thus their  plan won  out the decision to strike south was  accelerated in 1941 when the usa placed  an oil embargo against japan  something that the americans said would  only end if japan abandoned its war with  china and its gains in indochina  surely japan's southern ambitions could  be fulfilled without dragging the us  into war right  well no whilst the u.s public was  against intervention roosevelt made it  clear that much of the region was under  u.s protection  so why attack pearl harbor especially  given the size of america's navy in its  unmatched industrial base  well to be clear the japanese did not  believe that they could win a long  drawn-out war against the united states  and so the japanese government decided  that since war with the u.s was  inevitable that victory needed to be won  with a single decisive strike which  would [ __ ] the us navy  the idea was that with the u.s navy  crippled it would allow time for south  asia to be conquered  and such events would then knock  american confidence so badly that its  people would sue for peace  not everybody in the japanese government  thought that this was a good idea though  and many made clear that they would lose  any war long or short with the united  states they did it anyway and we all  know what happened  and the reasons were simple they felt  that they had to and importantly  they thought that they'd win i hope you  enjoyed this episode and thank you for  watching with a special thanks to my  patrons  james bissonette sam kelly money maker  the pastry section danny maloney moe  marvin cassell rob waterhouse james  castaneda jordan longley spinning three  plates gustav swan jerry lamdin rasheed  ali marcus asner izzy maggie paxkowski  cooling castleman david silverman  coppertone anthony beckett matthew  shipley  lexi schwinn robert wetzel skye  chappelle winston cayward aaron the  white spencer lightfoot and fobo joe
 

